{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Abram passed through the land unto the place of Shechem</b> I shall tell you a general principle that you will understand in all the pericopes concerning Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and it is a great matter. Our Sages mentioned it in brief and said (Tanḥuma 9): everything that happened to the fathers is a sign for the children. Therefore the verses elaborate on the account of the journeys and the digging of the wells and the rest of the incidents, and one who calculates about them might think they are superfluous things with no benefit — but all of them come to teach about the future, for when the event comes to a prophet through one of the three fathers, he will discern from it the decreed thing that is to come upon his seed. And know that every decree of the Watchers, once it goes from the potential of the decree into actual fulfillment, the decree is fulfilled in any event. Therefore the prophets perform an act with their prophecies, as in Jeremiah's statement when he commanded Baruch: \"And it shall be, when you finish reading the words of this book, that you shall tie a stone to it and cast it into the midst of the Euphrates, and you shall say, Thus shall Babylon sink,\" etc. (Jeremiah 51:63–64), and likewise the matter of Elisha when he placed his hand on the bow (II Kings 13:16–17): \"And Elisha said, Shoot, and he shot; and he said, An arrow of deliverance for YHWH, and an arrow of deliverance against Aram,\" and it is stated there (II Kings 13:19): \"And the man of Elohim was angry with him and said, You should have struck five or six times, then you would have struck Aram until it was finished; but now three times you shall strike Aram.\" Therefore HaKadosh Barukh Hu held Abraham in the land and performed for him foreshadowings of all that would be done to his seed in the future, and understand this. I now begin to explain the matters in detail in the verses, with the help of the Name:\n\n<b>And Abram passed through the land unto the place of Shechem</b> — this is the city of Shechem, for that is the name of that place, and Shechem son of Hamor was named after his city. Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 12:6): he entered into it as far as the place of Shechem, to pray for the sons of Jacob when they would come from the field in grief. And this is correct. And I add that when HaKadosh Barukh Hu held Abraham in that place first, before He gave him the land, it was hinted to him from this that his children would conquer that place first before they would merit possessing it — and this was before the sin of the inhabitants of the land was complete, so as to exile them from there. Therefore it says, \"and the Canaanite was then in the land.\" And when HaKadosh Barukh Hu gave him the land in a spoken promise, then he journeyed from there and pitched his tent between Bethel and Ai, for that is the place that Joshua conquered at the beginning. And it is possible that Scripture mentioned \"and the Canaanite was then in the land\" to indicate the context of the pericope, to say that Abram came into the land of Canaan and HaShem did not show him the land that He had promised, and he passed on as far as the place of Shechem — and the Canaanite, the bitter and hasty nation, was then in the land, and Abram was afraid of him, and therefore he did not build an altar to YHWH. But when he came to the place of Shechem, at Elon Moreh, HaShem appeared to him and gave him the land, and his fear departed, for he had already been assured in the land which He said, \"which I will show you,\" and then he built an altar to YHWH to serve Him publicly:\n\n<b>And Elon Moreh</b> — this is at the place of Shechem, and it is also called \"Elonei Moreh,\" as it is written (Deuteronomy 11:30): \"over against Gilgal, beside Elonei Moreh,\" and there are Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal in Shechem, near the Jordan, where they came at the beginning of their entry into the land. But \"Elonei Mamre\" is a place that is in the land of Hebron, far from the Jordan. And know that in every place where Scripture says \"Elonei Mamre,\" the name \"Mamre\" refers to an Amorite man who owned that place, as it is stated (Genesis 14:13): \"and he dwelt in Elonei Mamre the Amorite, brother of Eshkol and brother of Aner.\" And all instances of \"Elon Moreh\" and \"Elonei Moreh\" — that place is called by the name of a man whose name was \"Moreh,\" but he is a Canaanite from the land of the Canaanite who dwells in the Arabah. And when Scripture mentions Mamre without qualification, it is the name of the city, as it is stated (Genesis 35:27): \"And Jacob came to Isaac his father at Mamre, Kiryat Arba, which is Hebron,\" \"facing Mamre, which is Hebron\" (Genesis 23:19), for the man who owned the elon was named after the city. And likewise Shechem son of Hamor is named after the name of the city Shechem. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 79:8), according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, Mamre is the name of a place, and according to the opinion of Rabbi Nechemiah, Mamre is the name of a man."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The citation to Genesis Rabbah is given in the Hebrew text as (בראשית רבה מ\"ב:ח'), i.e., 42:8; the text as provided reads (בראשית רבה מ\"ב:ח'), but the content about the dispute between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Nechemiah regarding whether Mamre is a place-name or a person's name corresponds to Genesis Rabbah 79:8; I have rendered the citation as it appears, but noted the apparent discrepancy; the text as given says מ\"ב (42) which I have retained."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:28:09.234966871Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}